editorialMd:
Build xorValues

Start a stored number at 0. Read `readings` from left to right. Combine the stored number and the next number with bitwise XOR. XOR compares their binary digits: matching digits give 0, and different digits give 1. Use leading zeros when needed. For example, 3 XOR 5 is 6 because 011 XOR 101 gives 110. Record the stored number after each update.

Return the answer

Count the even numbers in `xorValues`. Store the count in `evenCount`. A number is even when it divides by 2 with no remainder. Complete `xorRegisterEvenKeyCensus` so it returns `evenCount`. Return it, do not print it.

Why this works

Each value in `xorValues` follows task 1. The later tasks use only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(n); extra memory, including the helper list, O(n). n is the input length. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

javascript
function xorRegisterEvenKeyCensus(readings) {
  const a=readings; let out=[];
  let state=0;for(const x of a){state^=x;out.push(state);}
  return out.filter(x=>x%2===0).length;
}


